{"cuisine/israeli-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/israeli-cuisine","n":"Israeli cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Levantine cuisine","cuisine/levantine-cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Israel","intro":[["Israeli cuisine primarily comprises dishes brought from the Jewish diaspora, and has more recently been defined by the development of a notable fusion cuisine characterized by the mixing of Jewish cuisine and Arab cuisine. It also blends together the culinary traditions of the various diaspora groups, namely those of Middle Eastern Jews with roots in Southwest Asia and North Africa, Sephardi Jews from Iberia, and Ashkenazi Jews from Central and Eastern Europe."],["The country's cuisine also incorporates food and drinks traditionally included in other Middle Eastern cuisines (e.g., Iranian cuisine from Persian Jews and Turkish cuisine from Turkish Jews) as well as in Mediterranean cuisines, such that spices like za'atar and foods such as falafel, hummus, msabbaha, shakshouka, and couscous are now widely popular in Israel. However, the identification of Arab dishes as Israeli has led to accusations of cultural appropriation against Israel by Palestinians and other Arabs."],["Other influences on the cuisine are the availability of foods common to the Mediterranean, especially certain kinds of fruits and vegetables, dairy products, and fish; the tradition of observing kashrut; and food customs and traditions (minhag) specific to Shabbat and other Jewish holidays. Examples of these foods include challah, jachnun, malawach, gefilte fish, hamin, me'orav yerushalmi, and sufganiyot."],["New dishes based on agricultural products such as oranges, avocados, dairy products, and fish, and others based on world trends have been introduced over the years, and chefs trained abroad have brought in elements of other international cuisines."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"israeli-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Israeli_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Levantine cuisine","cuisine/levantine-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Zahav: A World of Israeli Cooking","ref":"cookbook/zahav-a-world-of-israeli-cooking","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Michael Solomonov and Steven Cook, 2015","note":"Recipes and stories from the Philadelphia restaurant Zahav.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9780544373280","suggested":true},{"name":"Sababa: Fresh, Sunny Flavors from My Israeli Kitchen","ref":"cookbook/sababa-fresh-sunny-flavors-from-my-israeli-kitchen","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Adeena Sussman, 2019","note":"Tel Aviv-based food writer's recipes built around the Carmel Market.","url":"https://penguinrandomhouse.com/books/562247/sababa-by-adeena-sussman-foreword-by-michael-solomonov","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":10,"links":[["Baba ghanoush","dish/baba-ghanoush"],["Bavel (restaurant)","dish/bavel-restaurant"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Hummus","dish/hummus"],["Lahoh","dish/lahoh"],["Sabich","dish/sabich"],["Sufganiyah","dish/sufganiyah"],["Taboon bread","dish/taboon-bread"],["Zahav","dish/zahav"],["Ziva (dish)","dish/ziva-dish"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Tahini","ingredient/tahini"],["Eggplant","ingredient/eggplant"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"],["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Chickpea","ingredient/chickpea"],["Cornmeal","ingredient/cornmeal"],["Self-rising flour","ingredient/self-rising-flour"],["Sorghum flour","ingredient/sorghum-flour"],["Amba","ingredient/amba"],["Amba (condiment)","ingredient/amba-condiment"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Hummus","ingredient/hummus"],["Israeli salad","ingredient/israeli-salad"],["Laffa","ingredient/laffa"],["Parsley","ingredient/parsley"],["Pita","ingredient/pita"],["Salad","ingredient/salad"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Laminated dough","ingredient/laminated-dough"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Levantine cuisine","cuisine/levantine-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Within Israeli cooking","count":1,"links":[["Ancient Israelite cuisine","cuisine/ancient-israelite-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/mapuche-foodways":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/mapuche-foodways","n":"Mapuche foodways","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, Araucanía, south-central Chile, and southwestern Argentina","names":"Mapuche","intro":[["Mapuche cooking follows the seasons, built on grains and legumes gathered through the year. In the Araucanía, piñones, the big seeds of the araucaria tree, are cooked, sautéed with garlic, oregano and salt, and finished with ",["Merkén","ingredient/merken"],", smoked chile ground with spices. Spring brings digüeñes, chewy mushrooms that grow on oak trees, for salads, stews and empanadas, and maqui berries go into jams and drinks."],["Tortillas are baked under hot ashes, mültrün is a long wheat bread eaten with honey or jam, and muday, a lightly fermented grain drink, is part of the nguillatún ceremony. In Curarrehue, near Pucón, Ana Epulef, who learned from her mother and grandmother, has run the restaurant and school Mapu Iyagl since 2003, and in Santiago José Luis Calfucura opened a Mapuche restaurant in 2015."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Marca Chile, Gastronomía, alimentación de los mapuches","url":"https://www.marcachile.cl/en/gastronomia-alimentacion-de-los-mapuches/"},{"name":"Food Republic, Chile, breaking bread with the Mapuche","url":"https://www.foodrepublic.com/2012/04/11/chile-breaking-bread-with-the-mapuche-tribe"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"mapuche-foodways"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"ingredient/liqueur":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/liqueur","n":"Liqueur","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["A liqueur or cordial is an alcoholic drink composed of spirits (often rectified spirit) and additional flavorings such as sugar, fruits, herbs, and spices. Often served with or after dessert, they are typically heavily sweetened and un-aged, beyond a resting period during production, when necessary, for their flavors to mingle."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-liqueur"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liqueur"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine",[["Cassatella di sant'Agata","dish/cassatella-di-sant-agata"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Candied peel","ingredient/candied-peel"],["Ceramic glaze","ingredient/ceramic-glaze"],["Fruit juice","ingredient/fruit-juice"],["Icing","ingredient/icing"],["Marzipan","ingredient/marzipan"],["Ricotta","ingredient/ricotta"],["Sponge cake","ingredient/sponge-cake"],["Succade","ingredient/succade"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/9/92/Licor-Beir%C3%A3o-%26-Orus_-_Orus_Clothing_%28cropped%29.png","w":500,"h":551,"by":"Poit99","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Licor-Beir%C3%A3o-%26-Orus_-_Orus_Clothing_(cropped).png"}},"ingredient/five-spice-powder":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/five-spice-powder","n":"Five-spice powder","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Five-spice powder is a spice mixture of five or more spices—commonly star anise, cloves, Chinese cinnamon, Sichuan pepper, and fennel seeds—used predominantly in almost all branches of Chinese cuisine. The five flavors of the spices reflect the five traditional Chinese elements (wood, fire, earth, metal, and water) and flavors (sweet, bitter, sour, salty, and savory). The addition of eight other spices creates thirteen-spice powder (十三香), which is used less commonly."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-five-spice-powder"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Five-spice_powder"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Cantonese cuisine","cuisine/cantonese-cuisine",[["Char siu","dish/char-siu"]]],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",[["Tea egg","dish/tea-egg"]]],["Taiwan cuisine","cuisine/taiwan-cuisine",[["Tea egg","dish/tea-egg"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Dark soy sauce","ingredient/dark-soy-sauce"],["Fermented bean curd","ingredient/fermented-bean-curd"],["Fortified wine","ingredient/fortified-wine"],["Hoisin sauce","ingredient/hoisin-sauce"],["Honey","ingredient/honey"],["Maltose","ingredient/maltose"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Rice wine","ingredient/rice-wine"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Tea","ingredient/tea"]]}]},"dish/cimpa":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/cimpa","n":"Cimpa","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Indonesian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Cimpa are a variety of related cakes cooked by the Karo of North Sumatra, made of rice flour, coconut and palm sugar."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-cimpa"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cimpa"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Daily Cooking Quest","ref":"site/dailycookingquest-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dailycookingquest.com","note":"Indonesian home cook Anita's blog with a large archive of Indonesian recipes